rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))

q('A septic patient has a large liquefied liver lesion with a rim-enhancing wall. Which initial management direction is appropriate for suspected pyogenic abscess?', 'Cultures, prompt appropriate antibiotics and source-control assessment for image-guided drainage',[
 ('Antibiotics are never needed if drainage is possible','Drainage and antimicrobial therapy are complementary.'),('Drainage is never needed regardless of size or response','A large or poorly responding collection often requires source control.'),('Steroids as routine first-line treatment','They do not treat the bacterial collection.')],
 'Control both the organisms and the infected compartment', '**A pyogenic abscess is usually managed with antimicrobials and drainage when feasible and indicated.**', ['Obtain blood and aspirate cultures when possible without delaying urgent care.','Empiric coverage depends on source, severity and local resistance.'], '**Look for the route of infection.**', ['Biliary obstruction, portal infection and hematogenous spread can require separate treatment.','A persistent source can cause failure despite an apparently suitable antibiotic.'], [['Antibiotics','Treat pathogens'],['Drainage','Source control'],['Underlying source','Prevent persistence/recurrence']], [PY], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has liver abscesses with an obstructed infected biliary tree. What additional action may be needed beyond abscess antibiotics?', 'Biliary drainage and treatment of the obstructing cause',[
 ('Ignore the duct because any antibiotic permanently cures obstruction','Antimicrobials cannot mechanically relieve obstruction.'),('Repeated liver biopsy as the only source-control strategy','Biopsy does not drain the infected bile duct.'),('Delay all drainage until a fixed six-week period has elapsed','Cholangitis can require urgent source control.')],
 'The abscess may be downstream of another infected compartment', '**An obstructed biliary source can maintain sepsis.**', ['ERCP or another drainage route depends on anatomy and stability.','Abscess drainage may still be separately required.'], '**Treat the causal lesion as well as the current infection.**', ['Stones, strictures or malignancy need appropriate follow-up.','Culture data can refine antimicrobial therapy.'], [['Biliary source','Duct drainage assessment'],['Liver collection','Separate drainage decision'],['Definitive cause','Stone/stricture/tumor management']], [PY], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A diabetic patient has monomicrobial Klebsiella liver abscess and reports new eye pain and blurred vision. What complication requires urgent assessment?', 'Metastatic endophthalmitis',[
 ('A benign medication effect assumed without examination','Vision-threatening metastatic infection must be excluded.'),('Gilbert syndrome','This does not explain an acute ocular syndrome.'),('An expected harmless manifestation of every liver abscess','Eye symptoms are not a reason for reassurance.')],
 'An invasive infection can seed distant organs', '**Klebsiella liver abscess can be associated with metastatic ocular and CNS infection.**', ['Urgent ophthalmological assessment is needed for concerning visual symptoms.','Source control and systemic therapy remain necessary.'], '**Do not wait for abdominal recovery before addressing the eye.**', ['Permanent visual damage can occur rapidly.','Other neurological or focal symptoms also require targeted evaluation.'], [['Eye symptoms','Urgent endophthalmitis assessment'],['Liver source','Drain/control'],['Other focal signs','Assess metastatic sites']], [PY], 'clinical_scenario')
q('What should guide antibiotic narrowing after drainage of a pyogenic liver abscess?', 'Culture/susceptibility results, the likely source and clinical response',[
 ('A fixed identical antibiotic for every organism and region','Pathogens and resistance vary.'),('Normalization of bilirubin alone as proof all bacteria are eradicated','This is not a complete infection endpoint.'),('Stopping after one dose because fluid was aspirated','Drainage does not automatically eliminate all tissue infection.')],
 'An initial empiric regimen should become a targeted plan', '**Blood and abscess microbiology can identify the relevant organisms.**', ['Some cultures are negative after prior antibiotics.','Biliary and portal sources can have different microbiological patterns.'], '**Duration and route depend on response and source control.**', ['Follow symptoms, inflammatory markers and appropriate imaging.','Avoid using a residual cavity alone as proof of uncontrolled viable infection.'], [['Initial regimen','Source/severity based'],['Narrowing','Microbiology plus response'],['Completion','Integrated clinical assessment']], [PY])
q('An apparently cryptogenic pyogenic liver abscess has resolved. Why may age- and risk-appropriate colon evaluation be relevant?', 'An occult colorectal lesion can be a possible portal-entry source in selected cryptogenic cases',[
 ('Every liver abscess proves colorectal cancer','The association is not deterministic.'),('A normal liver enzyme profile excludes colonic pathology','It does not assess the colon.'),('Colonoscopy is required during uncontrolled shock before resuscitation','Timing must account for clinical recovery and safety.')],
 'An unexplained portal infection can warrant a search for an underlying lesion', '**Cryptogenic abscess has an observed association with colorectal neoplasia in some cohorts.**', ['Consider prior screening, symptoms and individual risk.','This does not replace assessment for biliary or other sources.'], '**Plan follow-up after stabilization.**', ['Explain the rationale without labeling the patient as having cancer.','Use the appropriate diagnostic/screening pathway.'], [['Cryptogenic source','Consider occult portal-entry lesion'],['Cancer association','Not proof'],['Timing','After safe stabilization']], [ref('Incidence of Colorectal Cancer in Patients Diagnosed With Pyogenic Liver Abscess','https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2023.48218')])
q('A traveler has a solitary right-lobe liver abscess and positive E. histolytica serology. Which statement is correct?', 'Amoebic liver abscess can occur without current diarrhea or a positive stool microscopy result',[
 ('Absence of diarrhea excludes the diagnosis','Extraintestinal disease may present without active intestinal symptoms.'),('All positive serology proves current abscess etiology in every endemic patient','Prior exposure and background seropositivity can complicate interpretation.'),('Imaging alone reliably distinguishes every amoebic from pyogenic abscess','The appearances can overlap.')],
 'Extraintestinal infection need not reproduce the intestinal phase', '**Integrate epidemiology, serology, imaging and the clinical course.**', ['Serology can be initially negative early in disease.','Pyogenic infection remains an important alternative, especially in sepsis or an atypical setting.'], '**Do not rely on one absent symptom or one positive marker.**', ['Stool testing can be negative.','Obtain additional testing or aspiration when diagnostic uncertainty or complication warrants it.'], [['No diarrhea','Does not exclude'],['Serology','Context dependent'],['Imaging','Not perfectly specific']], [AM], 'clinical_scenario')
q('What treatment sequence is required for amoebic liver abscess?', 'A tissue-active nitroimidazole followed by an appropriate luminal amoebicide',[
 ('Metronidazole alone as guaranteed eradication of all intestinal carriage','A luminal agent remains necessary.'),('Paromomycin alone for the invasive hepatic lesion','Luminal treatment alone does not adequately treat tissue invasion.'),('Oral vancomycin as standard antiamoebic monotherapy','This is not the correct drug class.')],
 'Treat both the liver lesion and the intestinal reservoir', '**Metronidazole or tinidazole addresses invasive infection.**', ['A subsequent luminal drug such as paromomycin clears carriage.','Clinical improvement of the liver lesion does not prove luminal eradication.'], '**Follow the symptomatic response.**', ['Failure to improve prompts reassessment of diagnosis, complications and bacterial superinfection.','Drug selection accounts for contraindications and availability.'], [['Tissue phase','Nitroimidazole'],['Luminal phase','Appropriate eradication agent'],['No response','Reassess']], [AM])
q('A typical uncomplicated amoebic liver abscess is improving rapidly on appropriate drugs. What is the role of routine drainage?', 'It is not routinely required in every responding uncomplicated case',[
 ('Mandatory surgical drainage for every lesion','Medical therapy is often effective.'),('Never consider drainage under any circumstances','Rupture risk, nonresponse or diagnostic uncertainty can justify it.'),('Drainage replaces all antiamoebic treatment','The infection still requires drug therapy.')],
 'Do not automatically copy the pyogenic source-control rule', '**Many amoebic abscesses resolve clinically with appropriate antiamoebic drugs.**', ['Imaging resolution can lag behind clinical recovery.','Large high-risk location, impending rupture or poor response changes the decision.'], '**Use intervention for a defined indication.**', ['A left-lobe lesion can threaten the pericardium.','An uncertain diagnosis may require sampling.'], [['Uncomplicated responder','Usually medical treatment'],['High rupture risk','Consider drainage'],['Diagnostic uncertainty/nonresponse','Reassess and possibly sample']], [AM], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which amoebic abscess particularly raises concern for rupture into the pericardium?', 'A large left-lobe lesion close to the pericardium',[
 ('Every tiny right-lobe lesion has the same anatomical risk','Location and size matter.'),('An isolated splenic cyst','This is not the stated hepatic anatomical relationship.'),('A normal liver with positive antibody only','That does not establish a rupture-prone lesion.')],
 'The adjacent compartment determines the complication', '**Left-lobe extension can create a dangerous pericardial rupture pathway.**', ['Size, residual tissue and imaging signs inform risk.','New chest symptoms or hemodynamic deterioration require urgent assessment.'], '**Plan drainage or surgery according to the complication.**', ['Antiamoebic drugs remain essential.','Do not wait for complete rupture when high-risk anatomy and clinical progression are evident.'], [['Left-lobe proximity','Pericardial threat'],['Right-lobe lesion','Different rupture routes'],['High-risk progression','Urgent intervention review']], [AM])
q('An amoebic liver abscess remains febrile and painful after several days of appropriate metronidazole. What is the best next principle?', 'Reassess diagnosis, bacterial superinfection, rupture risk and the need for image-guided drainage',[
 ('Assume persistent fever always proves drug resistance without further evaluation','Several alternative explanations exist.'),('Continue unchanged for months regardless of deterioration','A poor response needs reassessment.'),('Treat a residual cavity alone as proof that emergency surgery is always required','Clinical and imaging context determine the intervention.')],
 'Clinical nonresponse is a reason to reopen the model', '**An expected early improvement should be evident in many uncomplicated cases.**', ['A pyogenic abscess or superinfection can need a different treatment.','Impending rupture or other complications can require drainage.'], '**Use repeat imaging and sampling when they will change care.**', ['Do not infer persistent viable infection from slow radiological resolution alone.','Maintain the luminal-eradication component of a confirmed amoebic pathway.'], [['Poor clinical response','Reassess cause/complication'],['Slow cavity shrinkage alone','Not equivalent to failure'],['Intervention','Indication based']], [AM], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Ultrasound shows a hepatic cyst with a floating detached internal membrane, the water-lily sign. Which WHO stage is this?', 'CE3a',[
 ('CE1','This is an active unilocular cyst without the defining detached membrane.'),('CE2','This is the active multivesicular daughter-cyst pattern.'),('CE5','This is an inactive cyst with a heavily calcified wall pattern.')],
 'Stage the cyst before choosing treatment', '**CE3a is a transitional unilocular cyst with detached parasitic layers.**', ['CE2 and CE3b have a daughter-cyst/multivesicular architecture.','Stage, size, location and complications guide management.'], '**Do not treat every hepatic cyst as a simple benign cyst.**', ['Hydatid exposure and characteristic imaging matter.','Unplanned puncture can cause spillage or anaphylaxis.'], [['CE1','Active unilocular'],['CE3a','Detached membrane'],['CE4/CE5','Inactive patterns']], [WH,ref('WHO 2025 Cyst Staging Table','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK616291/table/ch1.tab1/')])
q('Under WHO 2025 guidance, what is suggested for an uncomplicated hepatic CE1 or CE3a cyst measuring 7 cm, with no biliary communication and suitable expertise?', 'PAIR combined with albendazole',[
 ('Unplanned aspiration without antiparasitic precautions','This ignores spillage, anaphylaxis and recurrence risks.'),('Observation as if every CE1 cyst were inactive','CE1 is active and the stage-size recommendation differs.'),('A bacterial antibiotic alone','This does not treat the hydatid parasite.')],
 'Apply a stage-and-size-specific recommendation', '**For uncomplicated CE1/CE3a cysts of 5–10 cm, WHO suggests PAIR with albendazole.**', ['Suitability includes location, accessibility and exclusion of biliary communication.','The recommendation is conditional and depends on expertise and resources.'], '**Use a planned parasitic-disease procedure.**', ['Provide appropriate antiparasitic cover and emergency readiness.','Follow the cyst after treatment for response and recurrence.'], [['Stage/size','CE1 or CE3a, 5–10 cm'],['Approach','PAIR plus ALB'],['Key exclusion','Biliary communication']], [WH], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Why is known cystobiliary communication an important problem for PAIR using a scolicidal agent?', 'The injected agent can enter bile ducts and cause chemical injury, including sclerosing cholangitis',[
 ('It guarantees that every scolicidal agent becomes harmless','Communication can increase risk.'),('It proves the cyst is no longer echinococcal','Hydatid cysts can communicate with ducts.'),('It removes all need for specialist planning','The complication makes planning more important.')],
 'An effective local chemical can be dangerous in the wrong compartment', '**PAIR is not suitable for every cyst anatomy.**', ['Assess imaging and aspirate/biliary findings through the procedural protocol.','Complicated communication can require an endoscopic or surgical strategy.'], '**Do not perform routine diagnostic puncture as though this were a simple cyst.**', ['Spillage can cause secondary disease or anaphylaxis.','Albendazole and procedural precautions are part of the plan.'], [['Scolicide in cyst','Intended local effect'],['Scolicide in ducts','Chemical injury risk'],['Communication','Alternative specialist strategy']], [WH,CD])
q('A hepatic hydatid cyst is inactive CE4/CE5, asymptomatic and uncomplicated. What management can be appropriate?', 'Imaging-based watch-and-wait follow-up rather than automatic intervention',[
 ('Surgery in every inactive cyst regardless of symptoms','Stage-based conservative management can avoid unnecessary harm.'),('PAIR as mandatory treatment of every calcified lesion','Inactive cysts have a different risk-benefit balance.'),('No follow-up ever because reactivation or complications are impossible','Monitoring remains part of a conservative strategy.')],
 'Parasite inactivity changes the intervention threshold', '**Selected inactive uncomplicated cysts can be observed.**', ['Confirm that the imaging classification is secure.','Symptoms, complications or stage change can alter management.'], '**Observation is a scheduled plan.**', ['Use serial imaging as recommended by the specialist pathway.','Explain warning symptoms and the reason to avoid unnecessary puncture.'], [['Inactive uncomplicated stage','Observation option'],['Complication','Reassess treatment'],['Follow-up','Imaging-based monitoring']], [WH,CD], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A hydatid cyst ruptures during an unplanned procedure. Which immediate and delayed risks are important?', 'Anaphylaxis and secondary echinococcal seeding',[
 ('Only a transient harmless bilirubin rise','The risks can be severe.'),('Guaranteed spontaneous sterilization of every spilled protoscolex','Viable material can seed secondary cysts.'),('A need to omit all antiparasitic follow-up','Specialist treatment and follow-up become important.')],
 'Spilled parasite material has both immunological and infectious consequences', '**Acute allergic collapse requires emergency management.**', ['Secondary intra-abdominal disease can emerge later.','A planned procedure uses measures to limit spillage and provide antiparasitic cover.'], '**Document and manage the exposure beyond the operating room.**', ['Specialist albendazole and other measures depend on the event.','Long-term imaging follow-up may be required.'], [['Immediate risk','Anaphylaxis'],['Delayed risk','Secondary cysts'],['Prevention','Planned containment and antiparasitic strategy']], [WH,CD])
q('How does alveolar echinococcosis typically differ from cystic echinococcosis?', 'It behaves as an infiltrative tumor-like hepatic lesion and often requires radical resection with prolonged albendazole when feasible',[
 ('It is always a single simple fluid cyst treated identically by routine PAIR','Its infiltrative biology is different.'),('It is caused by the same clinical form with no management distinction','E. multilocularis causes the alveolar form.'),('Antiparasitic therapy is never relevant','Long-term albendazole is central to management.')],
 'The parasite species changes the lesion biology', '**Alveolar disease can invade adjacent structures and mimic malignancy.**', ['Staging assesses resectability and extent.','Unresectable disease may require prolonged suppressive therapy.'], '**Use specialist multidisciplinary planning.**', ['Biliary and vascular complications can require additional care.','Do not apply cystic-stage PAIR rules to an infiltrative alveolar lesion.'], [['Cystic disease','Stage-based cyst strategy'],['Alveolar disease','Infiltrative growth'],['Treatment','Resection when feasible plus long-term ALB']], [CD,ref('WHO Echinococcosis Overview','https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/echinococcosis')])

